      "tool_use_id": "call_00_NQ8xTQ5DcCC3bx6IAbTo9904",
      "arguments": {
        "description": "Profile round 1 log2_",
        "subagent_type": "kernel-profile-analyzer",
        "prompt": "Profile measured round_num 1 of operator log2_ (in-place elementwise log2, Triton on Ascend910B4, msprof available) and return one actionable next experiment.\n\nContext of the measurement (authoritative eval round 1, all 30 workloads PASSED, geo_mean 0.5521):\n- small sizes (1024, 65536, 1048576 elements, all dtypes): candidate 12.1-13.8 us vs reference 7.5-9.9 us (speedup 0.59-0.76). Timing mode is `walltime`: median of (perf_counter around a loop of iters calls, sync only at loop ends) -> host launch cost is included and dominates these.\n- 4096x4096 = 16.7M elements: fp16 66.4 us vs ref 15.8 us (0.239); bf16 68.6 us vs ref 25.4 us (0.370); fp32 80.9 us vs ref 25.4 us (0.314).\n- Kernel: flat unmasked Triton kernel, one program per BLOCK, BLOCK=16384 for 2-byte dtypes and 8192 for 4-byte (BLOCK=32768 fails to compile: \"ub overflow, requires 2621440 bits while 1572864 bits available\"), float32 staging of the transcendental (`tl.log2(x.to(tl.float32))`), grid = cdiv(n, BLOCK) => 1024 programs (fp16) / 2048 (fp32), default num_warps.\n- Decisive question: on the 4096x4096 workloads, is the kernel bound by the MTE2/MTE3 memory path (bytes moved vs time, physical DMA counts), by the AIV vector/transcendental compute (Vector pipe active, log2 lowering), or by per-program/wave dispatch overhead (1024-2048 programs on ~20-40 cores)?\n\nPlease collect backend-native evidence (physical bytes, MTE2/MTE3/Vector activity, and if available the instruction listing for the log2 lowering) for log2_-timing-3 (fp16 4096x4096) and one small case such as log2_-timing-0 (fp16 1024) to quantify device-vs-host split, then give exactly one recommended next experiment with concrete parameter changes. Do not modify the candidate."
      }
    }
